  {
    "episode_id": 50313,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50313.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "In the state of Texas, there were 17 million plus voters registered. almost about 22 million that are eligible, but you got 17 million. Of those 17 million, 8 million turned out, the turnout rate effectively being 45%. But I want to narrow in on what was a chief concern in this past election and what it means now, which is border security, because you have said in a whole bunch of recent interviews that Abbott has blood on his hands. But many on the right, including former chair of the Republican Party of Texas, Matt Renaldi, claim Abbott won the election because of, not in spite of, his policy on the border. Voters across the Texas, in exit poll, after exit poll, repeatedly emphasized that the migrant crisis was the most important issue to them. And so if Abbott was imbued with this governing mandate, why then do you squarely put the blame and the blood on his hands?",
